Savlovschi Andrei-Bogdan, 311CB
120/120 points in local checker


I would like to start with specifying that most of the functions used for
queues, lists and stacks are taken and modified from the laboratory or copied
and  modified between themselves.

It is also worth mentioning that, as a suggestion from other homework's feedback,
I will describe what the functions do, and not how they do it.

First, the "main" is divided into 3 parts: initialization, command reading and deallocation. Since lists are used in all of the commands, I will present
the functions related to them in groups, followed by the
functions made for the commands.


1. LISTS

1.1 Functions that allocate new cells:
Alocate_Incident, Allocate_intervention, Allocate_units, Allocate_prio_cell,
Allocate_intervS_cell

Each of the functions named above require the information of the new cell to
be passed as a parameter. They check if the allocation succeeds and
return a pointer to that block of memory. For some, additional allocation
for the elements is required, like for the descriptions of the incidents.


1.2 Initialization functions:
Init_unitsQ, Init_Incident_List, Init_intervention_list, Init_interv_stack,
Init_prioQ

While most lists get initialized manually, the units list gets to be build
in it's entirety by the initialization, as there are a limited and known
number of them.


1.3 Functions that add elements to the lists:
Add_units_to_Q, Add_to_prioQ, First_in_prioQ, Add_to_interv_stack,
Add_to_interventionL

All of these function add a new cell at the back of their respective list,
with the exception of "First_in_prioQ", which places the incidents that got
postponed in UNDO_LAST_DISPATCH in the front off the priority queue. While
ADD_INCIDENT fits in this category, I will cover it separately.


1.4 Functions that extarct or remove cells:
Extr_unitQ, Extr_prioQ, Extr_interv_stack, Remove_intervention

As the category name says, the functions remove the cells from their respective
list, free the memory and return the information contained within the cells.
It is worth mentioning that the queues remove elements from the front while 
the stack and the intervention list remove them from the back.


1.5 Destroy functions:
Destroy_unitsQ, Destroy_prioQ, Destroy_interv_stack, Destroy_Incident_List
Destroy_Intervention_List

These functions remove the list with all of its cells, free the
memory allocated for them, and makes the pointer to the lists be NULL.


2.COMMANDS

2.1 ADD_INCIDENT

First, ADD_INCIDENT gets the information from input about the incident
and allocates a cell for it. A pointer to the incident cell is added to
one of the queues depending on the priority. Then, the cell is 
added at the end of the incident list.


2.2 CHECK_UNITS_AVAILABILITY

This command makes use of the "Number_of_units" function, which is also used
in DISPATCH. "Number_of_units" takes the units queue and counts them
by looping around the list.


2.3 DISPATCH

DISPATCH starts by checking the error cases (no units available or no
incidents to be resolved). Then it proceeds to extract the unit and an incident 
from a queue in the order of priority. The function adds the intervention
to both the list and the stack. At the end marks the incident as being
"intervened" and makes the unit unavailable.


2.4 UNDO_LAST_DISPATCH

Similar to DISPATCH, UNDO_LAST_DISPATCH starts checking the error case by
extracting interventions from the stack until it finds one that is
being "intervened". The incident goes back to having the "queued" status.

Depending on the priority, the pointer to the incident
gets to be put first in their respective queue. The unit is marked available
and the intervention is removed from the list.


2.5 SOLVED_INCIDENT

SOLVED_INCIDENT starts by searching the ID of the incident in the intervention
list. After the first loop, if the ID is not found, it will print an error
message (looking through the intervention list makes the function have less
parameters). If the incident is found, and is in the process
of being "intervened", it is marked as solved and the unit responsible is
marked as available. If not, an error message is printed.


2.6 SHOW_UNIT
2.7 SHOW_INCIDENT

Both of these commands search in their lists until they find the unit
or incident with the specified ID or complete one loop. If the ID is
not found, an error message is printed.


2.8 SHOW_INTERVENTIONS

This function goes through every element of the intervention list and
displays their information. If the list is empty it will print
"No intervention has been initiated".

2.9 list

"list" is an additional command I added by myself. It helps debugging
by showing the contents of the queues, of the stack, and of the incidents.
While it is not a necessary command, it will help in the presentation ensuring
that the program is functioning correctly. The output is displayed in the
console, so it does not mess with the checker or end up not getting displayed
in case of a segmentation fault.


I think this covers anything that the code does, if there are any
suggestions please do not hesitate to give me feedback. I want to know what I
could've done better in this Readme specifically.
(like personal opinions that would normally get mentioned).
Thank you for your time!
